<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cipollino-marble">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cipollino marble</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as marmor Carystium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cipollino is a green-and-white banded marble quarried in antiquity near Karystos in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Its onion-like layers gave it its Italian name. It was widely used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> for columns and wall veneers in buildings such as the Temple of Antoninus and Faustina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cipollino-marble.jpg" alt="Cipollino marble" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MarmoCipollino_FustoBasMassenzioRoma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Cipollino marble, also known as Karystian marble, is a variety of silicate marble. It has a white-green base crossed by thick wavy green bands, which are held between thin bands of mica, and its base and grain grow darker the further north the quarry lies. It is a metamorphic rock with crystals between 0.2 and 0.6 mm and veins coloured by epidote and chlorite.</p><p>The ancient quarries lay at several places on the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, between the modern towns of Styra and Karystos, and some of them survive with a mine-face of over 100 metres. A marble of similar appearance was also mined in the Iberian peninsula at the Anasol mines, in the Alpi Apuane, in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>First used in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the stone was exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the 1st century BCE. Pliny the Elder, in his Natural History, records columns of it in the house of the eques Claudius Mamurra, who had served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> as an engineer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>. The quarries at Karystos became imperial property in 17 CE, and in the imperial period cipollino became common throughout Rome.</p><p>It served above all for column shafts, many of them large and mostly smooth, as in the pronaos of the Temple of Antoninus and Faustina in the Forum. It was also carved: a crocodile in the Canopus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-villa">Villa Adriana</a> at Tivoli used its colour to imitate the animal's skin. Roman builders used it in the Basilica Aemilia, the Domus Augustana, the Villa Gordiani and the forums of Augustus, Vespasian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, and in rebuilding the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at Cyrene between 172 and 175. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> closed the quarries in 206.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The quarries were used again in the Byzantine period. Cipollino went into the Basilica of Gaza in the 4th century, the Church of Saint Demetrius in 412, the Basilica of Saint Apollinare in Ravenna in 504, the rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> in 537, the Church of the Holy Apostles in 546 and the Monastery of Saint Loukas between 920 and 945. It was also used for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the middle of the 11th century and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a>'s Basilica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1094.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cipollino marble” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its green-veined marble, <b>Cipollino</b>, was quarried heavily in Roman times.</blockquote><a href="/state/karystos">Karystos</a></li></ul></section>
